WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:02.100
[low, tense drone] A developer runs: npx playwright test

00:02.100 --> 00:04.400
One test passes: the happy path. Never tested: Auth0 "email already registered", an expired session, a declined card, a 503, stock selling out.

00:04.400 --> 00:05.100
1 passed · 5 scenarios untested · your customers will find them

00:05.100 --> 00:08.000
Your tests cover the happy path. Production has other plans.

00:08.200 --> 00:10.600
The testing gap. Unit tests can fake any scenario, but they mock the framework, so your code never runs.

00:10.600 --> 00:13.000
End-to-end tests run your real code, but you can't make Auth0, Stripe or a shipping API fail on cue.

00:13.500 --> 00:16.000
What if you didn't have to choose? [rising sound]

00:16.000 --> 00:22.000
[impact] Scenarist. Test reality. Control the rest.

00:22.300 --> 00:26.100
[beat starts] Your server calls Stripe, Auth0 and SendGrid. Real APIs won't fail on cue.

00:26.100 --> 00:29.600
Scenarist sits at the edge of your app and scripts those APIs per test: card declined, user exists, service unavailable.

00:29.600 --> 00:33.000
Your code runs for real. The world does what you script.

00:33.200 --> 00:37.500
Declare scenarios as plain data: a paymentDeclined scenario returns 402 card_declined from Stripe.

00:37.500 --> 00:41.000
In the Playwright test, switchScenario autocompletes every scenario ID. Type-safe.

00:41.000 --> 00:43.000
The test is written: switch to paymentDeclined, visit checkout, fill the email, click Pay, expect "Payment failed".

00:43.000 --> 00:47.600
Watch it run. Playwright drives a real browser: switchScenario, goto /checkout, fill the email field.

00:47.600 --> 00:50.800
Click Pay. The request hits your real server: middleware, cart validation, totals. Stripe is never called; Scenarist answers 402 card_declined.

00:50.800 --> 00:52.100
Your real error handling runs, and the browser shows "Payment failed". 1 passed.

00:52.100 --> 00:55.000
A real browser. Your real server. Only Stripe is scripted.

00:55.000 --> 01:00.500
One server. Every scenario. All at once. Six tests, each with its own test ID and scenario.

01:00.500 --> 01:06.000
Pro discount, free user, sold out, shipping down, card declined, and stock selling out mid-checkout all pass in parallel. 0 restarts.

01:06.000 --> 01:12.000
Request matching. Response sequences. Stateful mocks. Test-ID isolation. Production-safe, 0 kB. Next.js, Express and Playwright.

01:12.000 --> 01:16.000
Everything your backend can do. None of the ways it breaks.

01:16.000 --> 01:19.800
The full suite runs green, including the Auth0 and Stripe edge cases. 48 passed.

01:19.800 --> 01:22.000
Every scenario, on demand. Real backend code, every test. One server, zero restarts.

01:22.000 --> 01:30.000
Scenarist. Test reality. Control the rest. npm i -D @scenarist/nextjs-adapter @scenarist/playwright-helpers. scenarist.io
